[Music]  20 years ago the Nobel laureate Francis  Crick made what must have seemed at the  time a very bold claim this is what he  said you your joys and your sorrows your  memories and your ambitions your sense  of personal identity and your free will  are in fact no more than the behavior of  a vast assembly of nerve cells and their  associated molecules now when Francis  Crick said that in the astonishing  hypothesis you have to keep in mind that  at that time brain imaging wasn't it was  in its infancy that philosophers of mind  didn't care very much or didn't know  very much about what neuroscientists had  to say about how the brain worked but of  course now all of that's changed and  brain imaging has become an  indispensable tool and so we can peer  into the minds of people when they're  engaged in mental activities from  thinking about complex problems to  pondering their sense of self and  philosophers philosophers of mind  philosophers and neuroscience to become  intrigued with how the brain operates I  actually believe that we're on the  threshold of really coming to grips with  a biological understanding of  consciousness and this this question of  where consciousness comes from I think  is one of the most fundamental questions  one of the most intriguing questions in  science today one of our great mysteries  on a par with the origin of the universe  and so what I'm gonna say  right now in fact I'm just going to hand  off the microphone to my good friend  colleague and co-director Adrian Owen  who will tell you about our CFR program  on brain mind and consciousness and how  the brain gives rise to mind Adrian  thanks very much smell ah now what is  consciousness  I had a first blush that may seem like a  pretty simple question but think about  it for just a few seconds and I think  you realize it very quickly becomes  incredibly complex consciousness is  possibly the most intimate of human  mental experiences it's the one that  between us we find the most difficult to  describe to one another or to explain  let me tell you that consciousness is  the way that you are understanding what  you're hearing right now it's the way  that it provides the mechanism by which  you're going to remember the wonderful  dinner that you've had this evening it  provides the apparatus that I will allow  you to enjoy fine art and music and it  allows you to daydream about plans that  you might have for tomorrow basically  consciousness is is everything it  provides your entire view of the world  yet mysteriously it it disappears or  it's severely diminished every night  when we go to sleep and even more  worryingly it can be extinguished  completely and suddenly by something as  simple as a blow to the head so our big  question is how does this three-pound  lump of gray and white matter that you  all have sitting inside your heads give  rise to every thought feeling emotion  and plan that you have ever made and how  is it that it can go wrong in such a  multitude of different ways and the  answer to that question has huge  implications for many many fields not  only my own field neuroscience but also  education brain injury of course what  goes wrong when the brain is injured  artificial intelligence robotics  philosophy lorem policy essex psychology  even art music and creativity and  perhaps most importantly mental health  what we plan to do in our c-file program  is to bring together some of the  brightest minds on the planet to tackle  what I think are some of the biggest  questions remaining about how the brain  gives rise to consciousness let me tell  you about some of the questions that we  hope to address over the next few years  now first what is it that is so distinct  about human consciousness you're a ball  and watch nature programs in your scene  animals and it's clear that many other  species have many of the same  expressions and actions as humans but  but are those species conscious and if  so how is their consciousness different  to ours so one question I'm interested  in is basically what is it like to be an  ape right I'm sure you've all wondered  that why is it that other species do not  derive pleasure from music and art the  way that humans almost all do we know  that we can teach animals very  complicated sequences of actions animals  can perform extraordinary things they've  taught to do that or non-human species  but no species other than humans will  spontaneously tap their foot to a simple  tune why is that what have we got that  they don't have another question we're  interested in is how does consciousness  begin when I look at my one-year-old son  here scrutinizing the wheel of his his  stroller obviously he has consciousness  he's conscious but I I don't think his  consciousness is quite the same as mine  he doesn't have the same experience of  the world that I'm having right now most  of us agree I think that a fetus is not  conscious so somewhere between there  here and what's going on in my head  consciousness evolves it develops and  we're really interested in trying to  understand how that happens how does  consciousness develop and what is the  mechanism by which an infant or a young  child  starts to experience the way to us  experience the world in a way that we as  adults do supercomputers like big blue  and they can perform extraordinary feats  they can perform the very the most  complex cognitive feats perhaps better  in some cases than many humans if any of  you have tried to communicate with your  iPhone via Siri you may also think that  that phone has some kind of intelligence  right well is that consciousness is that  a kind of consciousness or is that  something else if as most of us believe  consciousness is a purely material  phenomena and then can we build a  machine that's functionally identical to  the human brain even insofar as having  some sense of consciousness or some  level of consciousness that's not a  question we're really interested in what  about so-called disorders of  consciousness now this is the field that  I spend most of my time working in can  patients who are in a vegetative state  or a coma following a serious brain  injury can they Harbor islands of  residual consciousness  despite overtly appearing to be  behaviorally non-responsive and we know  from look in my own lab that the answer  to that is yes they can and we can  detect those reduced levels of  consciousness using very sophisticated  brain imaging techniques  neuropsychiatric disorders like  schizophrenia and autism are also often  characterized by changes in  consciousness this time not in the level  of consciousness but in the contents of  consciousness think about things like  hallucinations and delusions those are  changes clearly changes in the cont the  conscious perceptions of the patient and  recent recent advances in cognitive  neurosciences are starting to shed light  on how this happens what changes in the  brain are necessary to induce these  unfortunate alterations in the contents  of consciousness and by learning more  about the biological underpinnings of  higher cognitive functions of these  complex cognitive processes we're going  to be able to identify precursors of  other diseases neurodegenerative  diseases alike like alzheimers disease  and Parkinson's disease what about drugs  you'll know about drugs prescription  drugs and recreational drugs are very  capable and often do induce so-called  alterations in consciousness changes in  consciousness think sedation is an  obvious example but also increases in  creativity increases in arousal  disinhibition many drugs produce many  changes that many behavioral changes  that suggest changes again in the  contents or the level of consciousness  it's interesting I think that in  different cultures these changes are  sometimes thought of as being  constructive and sometimes destructive  it depends where you are at what you  think about these these changes  particularly for example in changes in  creativity in response to recreational  drugs recent brain imaging studies are  starting to shed light on how it is that  drugs induce changes in the brain  typically changes in the  neurotransmitters of the brain and how  these go these changes go on to induce  the behavioural changes that we know  characteristic of drug use and finally  how is it that all of us are sitting in  this room are able to enjoy looking at a  piece of fine art or sway together to  the sound classic symphony or indeed cry  at the the same point of our favorite  movie is that some kind of collective  consciousness is there something that  binds us together and allows us to share  these inner mental experiences share  with each other and describe what  they're like we're interested in the  concept of collective consciousness now  these are all really big questions and  to address those really big questions  we're going to get some really big  people and those really big people are  gonna come from many different fields so  we're assembling a multidisciplinary  team and this is a truly  multidisciplinary program it will take  people from cognitive neuroscience my  own field but also from medicine we'll  use what will involve neurologists and  psychiatrists anesthesiologists  obviously are involved in changes in the  level of consciousness but also we want  we want to involve ethicists and  philosophers and psychologists  anthropologists people who have other  views of consciousness and this is I  think really where this program could  only be funded by CFR because this group  of people are not people that usually  meet each other let alone talk to one  another we're going to put them all in a  room and we're going to get them all  working on the same problem which is how  does the brain give rise to  consciousness  so in summary we're going to take the  most recent advances cutting-edge ideas  in areas like cognitive neuroscience  philosophical thinking computational  modeling and we're going to push all  this towards the solution of a single  problem which is how does consciousness  arise from the neural machinery of the  brain in doing so I do think this is a  new frontier for C far I think we're  moving to a point where we will be able  to answer the question of what is the  relationship between brain and mind and  essentially I think what that addresses  is what is it that really makes us human  thank you very much  [Applause]  [Music]

